  "summary": "The health-tech industry is navigating a critical inflection point as artificial intelligence adoption accelerates while healthcare's financial pressures intensify. Major AI companies like OpenAI and Anthropic are preparing for public markets with expanding healthcare footprints, while oncology and sepsis detection have emerged as particularly demanding test cases for AI algorithms, revealing how medical data complexity can undermine model performance. The healthcare sector faces a structural reckoning around 2030, with credit analysts warning that demographic shifts, reimbursement cuts, and AI disruption will fracture providers into winners and losers, prompting urgent focus on payment system resilience, EHR cybersecurity coordination between DoD and VA, and fraud prevention mechanisms that intervene before losses occur. Beyond technology, several stories underscore tensions between institutional guidance and clinical practice—the American College of Obstetricians and Gynecologists diverging from CDC vaccine recommendations, debates emerging over opioid addiction medications, and growing recognition that restoring patient-clinician relationships may matter more than adding digital touchpoints. Internationally, South Korea is expanding telemedicine for foreign patients while developing generative AI for drug efficacy prediction, and European countries face mounting U.S. pressure on drug pricing that threatens to reshape pharmaceutical markets on both continents.",
